# Last-handled neat-core version.
#
# This crate consumes neat-core via an unpinned `path` dependency that tracks head.
# CI (`scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh`) fails when the sibling neat-core
# presents a breaking bump above this baseline — pre-1.0 a higher minor, or any
# higher major — forcing a deliberate upgrade. Clear the gate by updating
# `backpropagation` for the breaking change and bumping this version to match
# neat-core.
#
# 0.9.10 -> 0.10.0: acknowledge neat-core #569 (GRQ#4257), which retyped the
# public `MemeticExport::weights` field from
# `BTreeMap<String, Vec<MemeticWeightExport>>` to the new `MemeticWeights` enum
# so both valid wire forms parse. `backpropagation` never names `MemeticExport`
# or any memetic weight type — it hands creature JSON straight to neat-core —
# so no code change is required. Verified by a clean `cargo build --release`
# and the full test suite passing against sibling neat-core 0.10.0.
#
# 0.10.0 -> 0.11.2: acknowledge two neat-core bumps.
#   * 0.11.0 (neat-core #619, "Milestone: Pruning") is additive — new
#     `prune_*` modules and parity fixtures; nothing this crate names changed.
#   * 0.11.2 (neat-core #626, Issue #613) split every kernel re-exported from
#     `neat_core::simd` in two: the existing safe name now validates the span
#     and falls back to the checked scalar path, and a new `*_unchecked`
#     `unsafe fn` twin carries the out-of-bounds precondition as a `# Safety`
#     contract. That is the breaking half, and `backpropagation` never names
#     `neat_core::simd` or any kernel — it reaches neat-core only through
#     `CreatureExport`, `compile_creature`, `creature_validate`, the propagate
#     and MSE entry points and the gene-facet helpers — so no code change is
#     required. Verified against sibling neat-core 0.11.2 by a clean
#     `cargo build --all-targets`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`
#     and the full test suite (17 test binaries, 0 failures).
#
# 0.11.2 -> 0.11.3: acknowledge neat-core #628 (Issue #614), which reworked
# `bump-deps.sh` so a per-crate `cargo update` cannot drag a quarantined crate
# past the release-age window. The bump touches no Rust source at all — the
# diff `0.11.2..0.11.3` is `bump-deps.sh`, its bats tests, `SECURITY.md`, a PR
# summary and the version line itself — so there is no API surface for
# `backpropagation` to name and no code change is required. A patch bump would
# have cleared the gate on drift alone; it is recorded here so the baseline
# states the version actually reviewed. Verified against neat-core `Develop`
# at 0.11.3 by a full `./quality.sh` run (issue #141).
#
# Deliberately NOT bumped to 0.12.0: that version exists only on an unmerged
# neat-core branch. Acknowledging it here would pre-silence this gate for a
# breaking change that has not landed — the gate compares against neat-core's
# `Develop`, which is what CI builds (issue #141).
#
# 0.11.3 -> 0.13.0: acknowledge neat-core #633 (Issue #625, 0.12.0) — now merged
# to `Develop`, which lifts the deliberate hold above — which made every
# `CompiledNetwork` field private behind borrow-only accessors so safe code can
# no longer edit a validated network into an out-of-bounds SIMD read, and
# 0.13.0 (#637, the security-scan milestone roll-up; nothing this crate names
# changed). `backpropagation` read `activations` and `hint_values_buffer`
# directly in `propagate_layout.rs`; those reads now go through
# `activations()` and `hint_values()`. Unhandled, the same bump took the GRQ
# fleet down through rust_scorer (GRQ#4724); this crate was next in line
# because GRQ builds it from the same sibling neat-core at head. Verified by
# `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` and the full test suite against
# the sibling clone at 0.13.0 (59eb6e6).
#
# 0.13.0 -> 0.15.7: acknowledge two breaking neat-core minors and the patches
# between them.
#   * 0.14.0 (neat-core #640, Issue #622) bounds the DECLARED observation width
#     before it is walked. `CreatureExport::input` is a declared count with no
#     backing data, and `compile_creature`, `validate_creature_topology` and
#     `cleanup_creature_with` each built one owned String UUID per declared
#     input before anything bounded it, so a sub-100-byte creature declaring
#     `"input": 100000000` allocated a hundred million map entries. The ceiling
#     now lives in `validate_creature_width`, and a declared input above
#     MAX_NODE_COUNT is refused as `CreatureError::TooManyNodes` before any
#     allocation is sized by it.
#   * 0.15.0 (neat-core #653, Issue #601) adds an underflow guard to
#     `CompiledNetwork::new` so a malformed buffer header can no longer abort
#     the WASM module.
#   * The patches in between are additive or internal: 0.15.x adds the
#     `propagate_codec` size-check overflow fix (#654, Issue #602),
#     `MemeticExport::prune_to` width bounding (#652, Issue #650), length
#     validation on the WASM-exported parallel-array batch functions (#666,
#     Issue #658), a CI exit-status fix (#649, Issue #634) and Deno workflow
#     plus documentation work.
#
# Neither breaking change alters a signature `backpropagation` names. Both are
# refusals on input this crate never constructs: it reaches neat-core through
# `parse_creature_json`, `compile_creature`, `creature_validate`,
# `propagate_topological_loop`, the MSE entry points and the
# `calculate_weight`/`calculate_bias` helpers, and it feeds them creature JSON
# whose declared width matches its own node data, never a raw compiled buffer.
# The tightened paths therefore only add error cases for malformed input that
# previously over-allocated or underflowed, so no code change is required.
# Verified against the sibling clone at 0.15.7 (de4b81b) by a clean
# `cargo build --all-targets`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` and
# the full test suite (18 test binaries, 0 failures).
0.15.7
